# Exercise: Yielding Control
#
# `chan.yield` voluntarily gives up the current strand's time
# slice. The scheduler may run another strand before resuming
# this one. Under cooperative concurrency it's how strands share
# CPU time.
#
# Stack effect: ( -- )
#
# In a single-strand test there's nothing else for the scheduler
# to run, so `chan.yield` returns more or less immediately. This
# exercise just verifies you can call it the right number of
# times.
#
# Conditional loops in Seq are written as direct recursion: the
# word calls itself, with an `if` for the base case. (Seq's
# stdlib loops — `times`, `each-integer`, `integer-fold` in
# `std:loops` — cover counted iteration when you don't need a
# condition.)
#
# Your task: write `count-with-yields` — given N, call
# `chan.yield` exactly N times and return N unchanged.
#
# Stack hint:
#
#   ( N )
#   dup 0 i.>                       ( N Bool )
#   [ chan.yield
#     dup 1 i.- count-with-yields   # recurse with N-1, returns N-1
#     drop                          # drop what the recursion returned
#   ]
#   [ ]                             # base case: leave N on top
#   if
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: count-with-yields ( Int -- Int )
    # Your code here
    drop 0
;

: test-yield ( -- )
    5 count-with-yields

    # Do not edit below this line
    5 test.assert-eq
;
